examples.question_text:
Reflect $y=x+2$ in $y=x$. Give the new equation.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=7mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,<->] (-4.3,0)--(4.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,<->] (0,-4.3)--(0,4.3) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \t in {-4,-2,2,4} {
\draw[black] (\t,-0.08)--(\t,0.08);
\path (\t,0) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\t$};
\draw[black] (-0.08,\t)--(0.08,\t);
\path (0,\t) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\t$};
}
\draw[black,dashed] (-4,-4)--(4,4);
\draw[curveBlue,thick,<->] (-4,-2)--(2,4);
\path (-3,4) node[curveBlue] {$y=x+2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
Interchange $x$ and $y$, then rearrange.
$\begin{align*}x&=y+2\\y&=x-2\end{align*}$
The intercepts $(0,2)$ and $(-2,0)$ reflect to $(2,0)$ and $(0,-2)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=7mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{curveRed}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw[black,<->] (-4.3,0)--(4.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,<->] (0,-4.3)--(0,4.3) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \t in {-4,-2,2,4} {
\draw[black] (\t,-0.08)--(\t,0.08);
\path (\t,0) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\t$};
\draw[black] (-0.08,\t)--(0.08,\t);
\path (0,\t) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\t$};
}
\draw[black,dashed] (-4,-4)--(4,4);
\draw[curveBlue,thick,<->] (-4,-2)--(2,4);
\draw[curveRed,thick,<->] (-2,-4)--(4,2);
\fill[curveBlue] (0,2) circle (1.5pt) (-2,0) circle (1.5pt);
\fill[curveRed] (2,0) circle (1.5pt) (0,-2) circle (1.5pt);
\path (-3,4) node[curveBlue] {$y=x+2$};
\path (3,-3) node[curveRed] {$y=x-2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.question_text:
Reflect $P(-2,3)$ in the line $y=x$. Find the coordinates of $P'$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=7mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw[black,<->] (-4.3,0)--(4.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,<->] (0,-4.3)--(0,4.3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,dashed] (-4,-4)--(4,4);
\fill[black] (-2,3) circle (1.5pt);
\path (-2,3) node[above left] {$P(-2,3)$};
\path (3.4,3.4) node[above left] {$y=x$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
Exchange the coordinates: $(x,y)\mapsto(y,x)$.
$P(-2,3)\mapsto P'(3,-2)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=7mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{imageRed}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw[black,<->] (-4.3,0)--(4.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,<->] (0,-4.3)--(0,4.3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,dashed] (-4,-4)--(4,4);
\fill[black] (-2,3) circle (1.5pt);
\fill[imageRed] (3,-2) circle (1.5pt);
\path (-2,3) node[above left] {$P(-2,3)$};
\path (3,-2) node[below left,imageRed] {$P'(3,-2)$};
\path (3.4,3.4) node[above left] {$y=x$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Interchange $x$ and $y$.
$y^2+x^2=9$.
This is the same equation as $x^2+y^2=9$, so the whole circle is unchanged.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=7mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw[black,<->] (-4,0)--(4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,<->] (0,-4)--(0,4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,dashed] (-3.5,-3.5)--(3.5,3.5);
\draw[black,thick] (0,0) circle (3);
\foreach \t in {-3,3} {
\path (\t,0) node[below right,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\t$};
\path (0,\t) node[above left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\t$};
}
\path (3.4,3.4) node[above left] {$y=x$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
